in dealing with  long-term schizophrenics of anybody in  the in the country with Bernie Reubens  help I was able to get that rule  and to bring at least after all those  years a certain amount of justice to his  situation the other case discussed in  that chapter is a case that my senior  partner Bert Jenner and I became  involved in after a physician had been  accused of failure to file tax returns  this was a orthopedic surgeon who was  literally a saint of a man some of you  might even remember his name his name  was was dr. Gavin and his case became  extremely controversial all over Chicago  and people on the south side of Chicago  in the south suburbs filed literally  thousands of letters and petitions and  so forth because this man had worked  1820 hours a day six or seven days a  week all of his life helping his fellow  man doing a tremendous amount of charity  work and so forth and yet because of a  an ill-advised prosecution and a  despotic judge his life was destroyed  and there was nothing that any of us  could do about it  and the result of that was as I say a  case of injustice because in fact it is  not a perfect system and we have justice  and we have injustice in the system  another case I guess the second case I  talk about in some detail is the famous  Chicago conspiracy seven case that those  of you that are of an age of most of you  in this room will probably remember that  was a case in which my role was was was  very minor but I represented along with  my partner Tom Sullivan a number of the  lawyers who were held in contempt for  that case but as a result I got to know  and observe the case and get to know all  of the defendants and so forth and I  think that the the chapter on that case  probably has some some interesting  stories about things that happened that  haven't ever been told before  and it also I think is perhaps an effort  to put that case into some context at  the time it was sort of the trial of the  century at least of the 60s and 70s that  was the trial of the century and and the  impact that it had on the the cultural  wars that were going on in that period  and the ultimate impact of the case in  really in preserving the First Amendment  freedom of speech when at the despite  the prosecution and the convictions in  that case the courts of appeals  ultimately reversed all of those  convictions and upheld the right of  protest and the right of these people to  protest the war at the Democratic  convention so that's a really  interesting case I think and the people  involved were truly fascinating people  there's another chapter that involves a  case a couple of cases but one in  particular that I tried representing  Madison Square Garden against the then  heavyweight champion Muhammad Ali and in  that case I think I really probably put  it in the book largely because of  Muhammad Ali's name hoping I'd sell a  few books but but but it was really a  fun case because the characters were  right out of Damon Runyon I mean every  witness in the case Don King was a  witness Ali himself was a witness  another fight manager named Eddie Futch  was a witness and and it one after the  other they they were all characters that  you would read about in a Damon Runyon  story and they and their testimony  sometimes was absolutely hilarious so  that's really kind of just a fun chapter  about some people you've heard of and  and maybe it was fun to read because it  the the people themselves were so  interesting after that I I talked about  a number of cases that that actually  occurred over a quite a long period of  the time I was practicing law because I  became involved in quite a number of  cases involving election  and political disputes and one of them  was a case that went up ultimately the  Supreme Court of the United States in  which we were able to establish that  that the patronage system i sued mayor  daley the real mayor daley and we were  we were able to establish that the  system of hiring and firing basic tirely  upon your support of that in that  instance of course the Cook County  Democratic Party was a violation of the  First Amendment and the Supreme Court of  the United States ultimately so held and  it was a fascinating case Tom Horne was  on the other side of it when we argued  it in the Supreme Court and and it was a  wonderful interesting argument  ironically about 15 or 20 years later  the present Mayor Daley and Tom Hines  who was then the Assessor of Cook County  we're having their patronage workers  fired by Jane Byrne because Richie Daley  was going to run for state's attorney  and so Tom Heinsohn Richie Daley hired  me to sue Jane Byrne to enforce the the  law that I had established by suing his  father and I thought that was a lot of  fun and and in fact when I what they  asked me to take the case I looked at  that then the now Mayor Daley had said  you know I really wasn't gonna do these  cases any longer but I can't resist  suing somebody on behalf for patronage  violations on behalf of somebody named  Richard Daley it was irresistible  another chapter involves the case that I  cared the most about of any case I ever  had it was called generally in this city  it was called the contract buyers League  case and it was one of the most  important civil rights cases of the  seventies one of the most controversial  and and some of you may remember it  involved we filed a lawsuit on behalf of  a class consisting of the black  in Chicago who had purchased housing on  contract and had been charged grossly  overcharged for the for the houses as a  result of the the segregation in the in  the housing market in Chicago that made  it possible with the tremendous influx  of blacks in the end of the city to to  overcharge them and it was a case again  that was you know front-page headlines  really for several years as the case  undeveloped and there was some  incredibly wonderful people who were  involved in that case and people that I  think I treasure that experience  probably more than any any case I was  ever involved in despite the fact that  in the end after a very very long trial  we lost the case after that I guess  another chapter involved my  representation of the people who were  alleged to be high up in the so-called  Mafia in Chicago and also in Milwaukee  and a couple of other places I tried a  series of cases for people whose names  ended in vowels and whoever thought that  Little John Tucker you know from North  Shore Country Day School would would be  representing these people some of them  were terrible people but some of them  were absolutely wonderful people and I  talked about two cases that I tried for  the two men who essentially ran sports  gambling in the Midwest one of them was  named Donald Angelini known as The  Wizard of odds and he essentially set  the line for all sports gambling all  over the Midwest and then his partner  and  childhood friend am cartina was the  operating man for for all of the  bookmaking people and they were really I  have to say two of my very favorite  clients they were much more reliable in  what they told you  than a lot of the corporate executives  that I represented their word was as  good as gold if they told you they were  that something had happened it had  happened they never lied to me and they  were delightful delightful men in the  course of representing them I was they  were they were prosecuted by what was  then known as the strikeforce  strikeforce against organized crime and  they had been targeted and as a result  of that that target those target  prosecutions we were able to establish  has happened so often in target  prosecutions that in fact the FBI had  manufactured evidence against them  flat-out lied under oath now and and and  manufactured documents well that kind of  thing is very very very difficult to  prove but we were really fortunate and  we were able to prove it  to to jut to the satisfaction of two  judges one of whom had been a United  States Attorney for many years another  one was had been brought in specially to  try the case from North Dakota an older  judge who had been the judge in Little  Rock Arkansas that that ordered the  federal marshals into Little Rock back  in in the desegregation days and he said  one of the and when he finally ruled  that the that the government had been in  fact guilty of manufacturing evidence  and threw the case out he said well he  said I think the prosecutor said well  well well what about you know what is it  that you think was not true about the  FBI agents testimony and he said well  there was so much of it it's a little  bit to try too hard to say exactly what  I thought if anything was true or  what was untrue but I'll tell you this  if J Edgar Hoover were alive today he'd  be rolling over in his mausoleum and if  he'd heard that man testify so that case  really all of the cases that I've talked  about it here in addition to I hope  being good stories involve lessons that  I learned along the along the line of my  practice and and one of them one of the  lessons from from those cases was the  problem of target prosecutions of and we  saw that later on in the star Clinton  situation then which resulted really  finally in the in the ending what both  the Republicans and Democrats realized  that things had gone to fire and they  ended the special prosecutor law and as  my wife reminded me today in in a sense  we're seeing the same thing now and I'm  sure all of you have read the or not  read the report but read in the papers  in the last few days of the Inspector  General the Justice Department's report  about the FBI problems in in the  terrorism investigations and when you  get I mean of course nobody could  possibly argue that we have to fight  terrorism but when you get to the point  where you have the FBI or the the  agencies that are involved so determined  to get somebody that they began  manufacturing evidence then then our  system is in grave danger and I think  that's a really important lesson finally  I guess the last chapter is a short  chapter in which I briefly I assure you  briefly pontificate on the subject of  our system of justice and and the  practice of law generally does the book  succeed in avoiding the problems of the  genre that I described at the beginning  I suspect that I am NOT the person to  make that judgment I will say that the  reviewers have been very kind so far  the reviews have been good and in fact  my favorite review I will quote to you a  portion of it Perkis lee the  pre-publication reviewers called the  book an eminently instructive guide for  law students and lawyers and for general  readers an authentic version of a world  they normally see only through the  meretricious lens of TV courtroom dramas  well that may be a little extreme but I  hope that it is something like that and  I hope that all of you if you have an  opportunity to read the book will agree  thanks very much for coming I'm really  there's so many old friends here that I  haven't seen for a long time that I've  just astounded and I want to see you all  after this I'll ask answer any questions  that you have and then when I'm done if  any of you have been kind enough to buy  the book I'll sit down someplace and  sign it and if you will tell me the  nicest thing you can think of you'd like  me to say I'll say it in the in the  court any questions I have a question  yes did you ever clean up your office  now that was an unfair question Ken  Burns used to be one of my law partners  and no I didn't there was you may recall  though Kenny at the time that I said I  was gonna leave the practice the Jim  mournfulness from the Tribune who was  their legal ready to wrote a long  article about how unusual it was for  somebody to leave at my age and so forth  in position and they came over from the  Tribune and took a picture of me that  ran the paper the next day and it had an  industrial-sized wastebasket next 