oing to indict a whole  lot more but these are very complicated  cases the law is frequently not I mean  this is not like murder or armed robbery  there are these are very complicated  cases and they're really to prove the  knowledge and intent of the highest  people the people that we're all waiting  to get indicted they're basically  working their way up the chain and that  is the way prosecutions in complicated  cases work they get some of the lower  guys that they can prove the case  against with direct evidence they indict  them they either convict them or make a  deal with them to testify about two  people the next line up and they work up  and I will I will confidently predict  you that for example that Ken Lay is  going to get indicted you may not have  been indicted yet but he's gonna get  indicted yes Carol how often do you wish  you were back in the courtroom never no  that's not true I really loved trying  cases but I from the time I was  graduated from college I made a kind of  a vow to myself that I wanted to spend  part of my time writing and thanks to my  wife I've had that opportunity and so I  don't regret it at all very poor this  year is I was in Hawaii you know I had I  didn't have a chance  yes ray John can you comment a now or  did you in the book on your relation  with opposing counsel in these various  matters I the reason for my question is  I see some of the younger attorneys  today that look at a litigated dispute  is Jenner and block against Sun and  shiner against Sidley or say and the  lawyers are battling the lawyers and for  getting about the issue that's it's up  it's a case about the client really did  you have you know respectful and cordial  relations there absolutely and and I  mean obviously there were exceptions to  that but that this of course we all have  well this is one of the real major  controversies in the profession now and  you know sometimes it's overstated I  think but when I was practicing law  basically the lawyers on the other side  for the most part were my friends and  you know when the case was over you went  out and you had a drink and  congratulated each other or whatever and  respected each other and it's hard  enough to try cases without turning it  into a pissing contest if you'll excuse  me and I do think that happens more now  and I don't know what can be done about  it the pressures of I guess you know  when when at any cost and they have have  turned it into it I think a more of a  jungle and that's really unfortunate but  I for me to say that it's really almost  unfair I hear them but I haven't you  know I haven't tried a case against  another lawyer to verdict in 18 years so  you know it's really more hearsay than  anything else let's talk about writing  john are you writing again and what are  you writing are you going to write  another book about the law or something  else yeah actually if you look at  yesterday's USA Today there's a an op-ed  piece that I wrote about the Michigan  affirmative action case but so I like to  write short things from time to time but  I am researching another book and it is  gonna be if I succeed in in getting the  cooperation I need to write the story it  will be another book about essentially  about the criminal justice system and  about a very really extraordinary series  of murder cases that I think  we'll create a book that really is much  wider than just a true-crime border but  I don't know that that'll work it  depends on some cooperation that I  haven't figured out yet but I've got a  I'm gonna keep writing absolutely and  probably mostly about legal subjects  because that's what I know the most  about sounds like you you think in  ethical terms that you want to bring the  Essex back to the legal profession is  that so or well I I'm not sure that  ethics has really ever left the  profession but I certainly do think  that's important I think ethics is  extremely important and you know lawyer  it's it's kind of energy lawyers have  always had a terrible reputation you  know I mean they're like congressmen  everybody hates congressmen except their  own congressmen and everybody hates  lawyers except their own lawyers and two  or three years ago and and and of course  lawyers there they feel terrible about  this in there we're always trying to  find ways to make people like us you  know and you know and trying how did we  get this awful image and what can we do  about it  not sure this is I'm not sure I haven't  wandered off though your question but I  do have to say that I I had a really  funny experience about four or five  years ago I was going through some  papers that I had that had been belonged  to my father who was a lawyer and who  had graduated from law school I think in  1926 or something like that would that  be right patty something close to that  and there was a speech in these papers  that he had written in 1928 and the  speech was about how can we get people  to like lawyers  so yeah it hasn't changed and I don't  think it'll ever change you know we're  in a profession where we represent  clients and the people you know on the  other side don't like us and they think  they read these terrible things but I  think it's a wonderful profession and  I'm very glad I was part of it okay this  is a follow-up question you're very glad  you were part of it you had a an amazing  career one of your reviewers said that  it would be impossible in the 21st  century for anyone to have a career like  the one that you had do you think that  that's right well and and and the reason  that the reasons that he gave for that  were reasons that everybody is concerned  about the tremendous emphasis that there  has developed on the bottom line lawyers  making huge amounts of money and  insisting that the young associates work  you know 22 or 2300 hours a year on  paying matters lawyers skipping from one  firm to another the lack of a cohesive  group of partners who will say to each  other yeah 